#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: rozum-ucc-chat
version: 3.0.0
description: rozum UCC — reactive ScalaScript toolkit chat app (Companion streaming + Agent). Built via emit-spa.
frontend: react
---
# rozum — chat (Companion + Agent)

A phone-first CHAT app, rewritten as a **reactive ScalaScript toolkit app** (like `center.ssc`) and
built with `emit-spa --frontend react`. Two modes over the same shared local model on control-serve
(:8411, Face-ID-gated):

- **💬 Companion** (default) — talks to the resident model DIRECTLY, token-by-token, over a streamed
  `POST /control/chat/stream` (OpenAI SSE piped from the gateway). Never hangs.
- **🤖 Agent** — runs a headless coding agent (claude/codex/opencode) in a project via
  `POST /control/coder/launch` + polls `GET /control/coder/log`.

## What is reactive-toolkit vs. the JS orchestration fence

The toolkit owns ALL the reactive plumbing; a single `javascript` fence owns the imperative turn glue.

- **Toolkit (declarative, this `scalascript` block):**
  - state — `persistedSignal` (mode/agent/model/project/ready/turns) + `signal` (draft/streaming/sendTick)
  - streaming transport — `fetchStreamSignal("/control/chat/stream", body, sendTick)`; the request `body`
    is a `computedSignal` assembled from the committed `turns` (so it is bridge-tracked → never stale)
  - status polling — `intervalTick(5000)` + `fetchUrlSignal("/control/status")` (the sysbar's data source)
  - config screen — `remoteTable` model/project pickers + `signalLabelButton` mode/agent chips +
    `showWhen` for the agent-only block and for config-vs-chat switching (`ready`)
  - i18n — `localeSignal` + `localeText` (reactive, no reload)
- **JS fence (imperative, the `javascript` block):** reads/writes those toolkit signals by name. It
  parses the accumulated SSE, commits finished turns into the persisted `turns`, drives the agent
  poll loop, and PAINTS the styled chat surfaces (sysbar row + bubble thread + composer) whose exact
  dark CSS and left/right bubble layout the toolkit's text/table widgets cannot express. The stream is
  carried by `fetchStreamSignal` (NOT a raw fetch); the fence only re-triggers it (bump `sendTick`,
  force a bridge sync) and reads the growing `streamRaw` signal.

### emit-spa gotchas honored here (all verified empirically)
- A `javascript` fence is spliced VERBATIM at module scope **twice** (once before the `const`s, once
  after). So the fence contains ONLY function definitions + a deferred `setTimeout(boot)`; NO top-level
  statement touches a toolkit `const` (a temporal-dead-zone reference blanks the whole page).
- Any signal referenced ONLY inside the fence is **tree-shaken away**. Every fence-touched signal is
  also referenced in the `scalascript` view (the hidden `_sink` computed anchors the fence-only ones).
- `fetchStreamSignal`'s `body` must be a `computedSignal` (auto-subscribed → its `_sv` bridge value
  stays fresh); a plain unrendered signal stays at its mount seed and the POST 400s on empty messages.
- Re-triggering a stream from the fence: bump the tick signal, then `dispatchEvent(new Event('hashchange'))`
  to force the bridge sync that fires the stream's tick subscription.

